pocket-motion-only: the equalised keys fill 0042's PATH TABLE, which only 0042's PATH op draws; upstream has no `<Path>`, so the change has nothing to land on there.

0046 — a morph between keys drawn separately. 0043 refused morph keys
whose commands differed after normalisation, because resampling chooses a
point correspondence nobody declared. This patch chooses one by a rule
written down here, in `framework/compiler/path-data.ts` and in
`patches/0045-path-primitives.design.md` in the motion repository, so the
correspondence is a function of the keys and nothing else, and any keys can
morph — an icon into another icon.

Keys whose commands line up are untouched: `compilePathEntry` first aligns
them as 0043 did (a line against a cubic is promoted), and only when that
fails does it call `equalizeKeys`. So every morph that baked before bakes to
the same bytes, and a single key never reaches the new code. In the motion
repository the only multi-key `<Path>`s are `compositions/vector-paths`'
two, and both key sets, with a line/cubic promotion, are pinned by hash in
its `tests/path-primitives.test.ts` as 0043 baked them.

The rule, in order (every key keeps every point it was written with; cubics
are only split, by de Casteljau, which leaves a curve where it was; a line
is a cubic with controls at 1/3 and 2/3):

1. Subpaths. Key 0 keeps its subpaths' order, start points and directions,
   so a trim or dash on key 0 is what was written. Each later key is paired
   with the key before it: closed subpaths with closed ones by area, open
   with open by length, largest with largest, ties in written order. A
   subpath with no partner pairs with a point at the centre of its own
   bounding box, so it grows out of or shrinks into itself; a later key's
   subpaths take the order of the slots they pair with.
2. Winding. If the largest closed pair winds opposite ways, every subpath of
   the later key is reversed; with no closed pair, the largest open pair
   decides by which way round its ends sit closer. A whole key or none of
   it: reversing one subpath can change what a nonzero fill covers, and
   reversing all of them cannot.
3. Start points. On each closed pair the later key's start moves to the
   point that minimises the summed squared distance between the two
   outlines at 64 equal fractions of arc length; the candidates are its own
   vertices and 64 equal fractions; the first of equal scores wins.
4. Points. On each slot every key is cut at every vertex any key has, by
   fraction of arc length (fractions within 1e-9 are one vertex, and a key
   that repeats a point there gets as many), so a corner of one key meets a
   point at the same fraction of the other and every key has the same number
   of cubics.

Arc length is measured on 16 chords a cubic, and a fraction becomes a
parameter by interpolating along those chords: that decides where a cut
lands on the curve, never whether a point is on it. + - * / and Math.sqrt in
f64 only, so the bytes do not depend on the host.

The one refusal left is the table's: more than eight keys.

Red cases, run by `baselines/path-primitives/redcheck-ts.ts` (an edit to one
`+` line of this patch, the vendored tree rebuilt from it and restored),
each turning the named test of the motion repository red, against its test
run green first (`baselines/path-primitives/runs/redcheck-ts.txt`):
- keys that line up are equalised too -> -t keys that already line up bake the bytes 0043 baked
- a split's second half from the wrong control point -> -t every key keeps its outline
- no start search -> -t one outline written another way morphs to itself
- no winding rule -> -t one outline written another way morphs to itself
- subpaths paired in written order, not by size -> -t one outline written another way morphs to itself
- a cut inside a cubic becomes a nowhere-cubic at its end -> -t one outline written another way morphs to itself
- an unpartnered subpath shrinks to the origin -> -t a subpath with no partner is a point
- arc length through Math.hypot -> -t nothing reaches the host's trigonometry

All 8 red (`tests/path-primitives.test.ts`), and `tests/catalog.test.ts` G9
probes an equalised verb list, an unpartnered slot and the eight-key
refusal.

